It says: "here is a loop that will continue as long
as curr is true", not at all the same thing in
programming as a general activity, and merely true
because of a particularly questionable design choice
in the C language in particular.  That is already a
massive amount of type confusion induced by your
idiom.

The variable curr is _incapable_ of taking on the
value 0; that is a type violation. Moreover, the
implementation requirements of C compilers do not
require that a physical NULL pointer be encoded as
"all bits zero", and in some implementations, they
are not.

The variable curr _is_ capable of taking on _an
instance of_ the value NULL, which happens, in some
but not all C implementations, to be implemented as
a register set to zeros, but that is purely a
coincidence.

The instance of the value NULL which it is capable
of holding is one of type "whatever kind of pointer
'curr' _is_". Again, C, an incredibly weakly typed
language, lets you do things as a result of that
weakness which the wise programmer decides _not_ to
do, like muddle types wildly in the code as written.

